From 798afa1a8dc2875267eb7fe26c731519c06e424e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler South <tsouth2@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:23:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Give Ghostty OpenGL 4.3 where virgl offers only 4.2

On some hosts, such as Intel UHD 770 (#383), virgl offers only an OpenGL 4.2
core profile although the Windows driver has 4.6. Ghostty asks for 4.3, its
EGL context fails with BadMatch and no window appears. With
MESA_GL_VERSION_OVERRIDE=4.3 and MESA_GLSL_VERSION_OVERRIDE=430 it opens and
draws correctly there.

Give Ghostty, and only Ghostty, those two variables when the renderer stops at
4.2. ghostty-gl decides once per login: on CPU rendering it does nothing,
otherwise a small surfaceless EGL probe finds the highest core profile the
renderer creates, and only 4.2 gets the override. The result lives in
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, so a new host GPU or render mode is probed again. A probe
that fails or times out saves nothing, so the next start probes again;
starts that race share one probe under a lock, and for ten seconds after a
failure they start Ghostty without probing. The probe needs only the render
node, not the compositor.

Launchers start Ghostty by D-Bus activation of app-com.mitchellh.ghostty.service,
which gets a drop-in reading that file after the probe unit. Everything else
(xdg-terminal-exec, the screensaver, typing ghostty, the unit's own
ExecStart) runs a bare ghostty, so /usr/local/bin/ghostty links to a wrapper
that applies the file and execs /usr/bin/ghostty. A pacman hook keeps the link
only while Ghostty is installed, so Omarchy still sees it as missing until it
is, and catch-up sets it at every boot for disks that installed Ghostty before
the hook. None of it is a file the package owns.

Compatibility revision 74 delivers it to existing disks.
